{"id":4591059,"accountId":29,"type":"text","title":"Rankings Watch: Zheng returns to Top 30, Andreeva makes Top 3 debut","description":"Beijing champion Zheng Qinwen returns to the Top 30 in the latest edition of the PIF WTA Rankings, while finalist Mirra Andreeva becomes the youngest player to make her Top 3 debut in 21 years. Elsewhere, Nikola Bartunkova, Alina Charaeva and Sun Xinran all hit significant milestones after making an impact in Beijing.","date":"2026-10-12T13:39:00Z","location":"","coordinates":[0.0,0.0],"commentsOn":false,"copyright":null,"publishFrom":1791812388160,"publishTo":0,"tags":[{"id":12742,"label":"Rankings Update"},{"id":4886,"label":"News"},{"id":4993,"label":"topic:rankings"},{"id":5029,"label":"topic:stats-corner"},{"id":5003,"label":"singles"}],"platform":"PULSE_CMS","language":"en","additionalInfo":{},"canonicalUrl":"","references":[{"label":null,"id":333616,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"333616"},{"label":null,"id":319696,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"319696"},{"label":null,"id":319641,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"319641"},{"label":null,"id":310440,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"310440"},{"label":null,"id":332718,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"332718"},{"label":null,"id":330364,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"330364"},{"label":null,"id":327061,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"327061"},{"label":null,"id":331809,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"331809"},{"label":null,"id":337668,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"337668"},{"label":null,"id":320228,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"320228"},{"label":null,"id":312328,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"312328"},{"label":null,"id":329198,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"329198"},{"label":null,"id":317964,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"317964"},{"label":null,"id":328120,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"328120"},{"label":null,"id":320313,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"320313"},{"label":null,"id":328767,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"328767"},{"label":null,"id":319183,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"319183"},{"label":null,"id":334883,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"334883"},{"label":null,"id":335958,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"335958"},{"label":null,"id":332168,"type":"TENNIS_PLAYER","sid":"332168"}],"related":[{"label":"How humility and hard truths restored Zheng Qinwen’s fire in Beijing","id":4590763,"type":"TEXT","sid":"4590763"},{"label":"Best of Wuhan media day: Top quotes and Kostyuk, Eala's adventures","id":4590461,"type":"TEXT","sid":"4590461"},{"label":"'Back to normal': Pegula ready for late-season push in Wuhan","id":4590642,"type":"TEXT","sid":"4590642"}],"metadata":{},"titleTranslations":null,"lastModified":1791815215778,"titleUrlSegment":"rankings-watch-zheng-returns-to-top-30-andreeva-makes-top-3-debut","visibilityOrder":[],"allowedUserGroups":[],"allowedTerritories":[],"body":"<p>The first WTA 1000 of the 2026 Asian Swing on the WTA Tour Driven by Mercedes-Benz ended with a record-setting title run as <a href=\"https://www.wtatennis.com/players/328120/qinwen-zheng\">Zheng Qinwen</a> became the first home player to claim the China Open crown since the tournament's inception in 1994.</p>\n<p>Zheng battled through four consecutive three-setters in her first four rounds, but did not concede a set in her last three matches -- which included a pair of Top 10 wins, over Elina Svitolina and Mirra Andreeva, to take her 2026 total to three. In total, she dropped 78 games en route to the trophy -- the most lost in any WTA 1000 or Tier I title run since the format's inception in 1990.</p>\n<p>Only one Chinese player had previously made the Beijing final -- Chen Li-Ling, who was the runner-up to Wang Shi-Ting in 1994. Zheng's title run was the 13th time a Chinese player had won a WTA tournament on home soil, the first since her own 2023 Zhengzhou trophy, and the first at WTA 1000 level.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n\n\n</div><p>The former No. 4's sixth title overall continued her remarkable resurgence over the past two months. After undergoing elbow surgery last July and attempting to return at Beijing last year, Zheng's comeback proper started this February in the Middle East Swing. She compiled an 11-13 record through Toronto, where she lost her first qualifying match to Lanlana Tararudee. Following that tournament, the 24-year-old opted for an intense training block in Florida ahead of the US Open -- and since then, she has gone 14-2, including 8-1 in three-setters.</p>\n<p>Just four months after her ranking fell to No. 160 in June, Zheng is already back in the Top 30. In the latest edition of the PIF WTA Rankings, she soars 31 places from No. 54 to No. 23.</p>\n<h4><strong>Andreeva becomes youngest to make Top 3 debut in 21 years</strong></h4>\n<p>Beijing runner-up Andreeva continued a career-best season which has already seen her become a Grand Slam champion for the first time by reaching her fifth final of the year -- a joint tour-leading number alongside Elena Rybakina, Aryna Sabalenka and Jessica Pegula.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n\n\n</div><p>At 19 years and 166 days old, Andreeva is rewarded with her Top 3 debut this week, climbing two places from No. 5 to No. 3. She's the youngest player to enter the Top 3 since a 17-year-old Maria Sharapova in February 2005. Since then, only two other players have cracked the Top 3 as teenagers -- Caroline Wozniacki (19 years and 205 days old in February 2010) and Coco Gauff (19 years and 182 days old in September 2023).</p>\n<h4><strong>Bartunkova enters Top 30, Charaeva makes Top 100 debut</strong></h4>\n<p>Two early upsets shook the Beijing draw, and the two players who dealt them out are both rewarded by hitting new milestones this week.</p>\n<p>Back in June, Nikola Bartunkova led Aryna Sabalenka 6-2, 4-0 in the Berlin quarterfinals, but was unable to close out the win. The 20-year-old Czech finished the job in the Beijing third round -- then went all the way to her first WTA 1000 semifinal, claiming another Top 10 win along the way over compatriot Karolina Muchova. Bartunkova, who ended 2025 ranked No. 130, climbs five places from No. 34 to make her Top 30 debut at No. 29 this week.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n\n</div><p>Alina Charaeva, the 2020 Roland Garros junior finalist, had already notched some career-best results in 2026 -- her first two WTA quarterfinals in Hamburg and Sao Paulo, her first WTA 125 title in Midland. But she had yet to defeat -- or even face -- a Top 30 opponent until her astonishing upset of new World No. 1 Elena Rybakina from a set and a break down in the Beijing second round. Charaeva went on to make the last 16, and this week jumps 20 places from No. 118 to make her Top 100 debut at No. 98. The 24-year-old becomes the second Armenian to be ranked in the Top 100 following Elina Avanesyan.</p>\n<h4><strong>Mertens boosted by Beijing run; Jovic, Kraus, Sun up to new career highs</strong></h4>\n<p>For nearly a decade, Elise Mertens has been a Top 50 mainstay: she first broke into it in July 2017, and has not fallen out since. The 30-year-old Belgian is taking another run at the career high of No. 12 she set in November 2018. At Wimbledon in July, she reached her first Grand Slam quarterfinal since the 2020 US Open. In Beijing last week, she made her first WTA 1000 semifinal since Madrid 2021.</p>\n<p>Mertens' pair of Top 10 upsets in Beijing over Coco Gauff and Iga Swiatek also take her total this year to four -- the most Top 10 wins she's ever scored in a single season. She climbs five places from No. 19 to No. 14 this week, her highest position since May 2021.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n\n</div><p>Beijing quarterfinalists Elina Svitolina and Karolina Muchova both move up one place, to No. 6 and No. 7 respectively. For Svitolina, it's her highest ranking since September 2021.</p>\n<p>Elsewhere, Iva Jovic and Sinja Kraus have both set new career highs after making the Beijing fourth round. Jovic, 18, is up to spots to No. 11 and Kraus, 24, jumps 20 to No. 75 after reaching the last 16 of a WTA 1000 event for the first time.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n\n</div><p>Junior No. 1 Sun Xinran made an impact on her WTA main-draw debut as a wild card. The Chinese 16-year-old, fresh off the US Open girls' title, upset Eva Lys and Cristina Bucsa -- the latter her first Top 50 win -- to make the Beijing third round. Sun became the first 2010-born player to win matches at WTA 1000 level, and this week she soars 164 places to enter the Top 500 for the first time at No. 418. She's now the second-highest ranked 2010-born player following Memphis champion Kristina Liutova at No. 116.</p>\n<h4><strong>Jeanjean, Shubladze, Sramkova, Aksu rise following WTA 125 titles</strong></h4>\n<p>Four WTA 125 events took place over the past two weeks, all on hard courts -- two in China (Jingshan followed by Suzhou) and two in Türkiye (Adana followed by Samsun).</p>\n<p>In Jingshan, Alexandra Shubladze added her first WTA 125 title to her haul of five World Tennis trophies this season. The 21-year-old's run culminated in fine style as she notched her first career Top 100 win over Jessica Bouzas Maneiro 6-3, 6-2 in the final, extending her 2026 record to 55-15. Shubladze leaps 31 places to a new career high of No. 132.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n        <aside class=\"instagram-post embeddable-widget embeddable-widget--floating embeddable-widget--centered-mobile \"><blockquote class=\"instagram-media\" data-instgrm-captioned data-instgrm-permalink=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeEY6kDAVHB/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" data-instgrm-version=\"14\" style=\" background:#FFF; border:0; border-radius:3px; box-shadow:0 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.5),0 1px 10px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.15); margin: 1px; max-width:540px; min-width:326px; padding:0; width:99.375%; width:-webkit-calc(100% - 2px); width:calc(100% - 2px);\"><div style=\"padding:16px;\"> <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeEY6kDAVHB/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" style=\" b