rgin-bottom:0; margin-top:8px; overflow:hidden; padding:8px 0 7px; text-align:center; text-overflow:ellipsis; white-space:nowrap;\"><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeHd56tMAjX/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" style=\" color:#c9c8cd; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; font-size:14px; font-style:normal; font-weight:normal; line-height:17px; text-decoration:none;\" target=\"_blank\">A post shared by WTA125 Adana Open (@adana.open)</a></p></div></blockquote>\r\n<script async src=\"//www.instagram.com/embed.js\"></script></aside>\n</div><p>In Suzhou, Rebecca Sramkova lifted the trophy -- the former No. 33's first at any level since Hua Hin II 2024 -- with a 6-3, 6-1 defeat of qualifier Kyoka Okamura in the final, and climbs 18 places to No. 134. Okamura, who lost a high-quality first-round tilt in Singapore to eventual champion Leylah Fernandez three weeks ago, has maintained her form since -- the Japanese 31-year-old made the Jingshan quarterfinals ahead of her first WTA 125 final, and vaults 59 places from No. 303 to No. 244 this week.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n        <aside class=\"instagram-post embeddable-widget embeddable-widget--floating embeddable-widget--centered-mobile \"><blockquote class=\"instagram-media\" data-instgrm-captioned data-instgrm-permalink=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeW6Gd9k8W8/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" data-instgrm-version=\"14\" style=\" background:#FFF; border:0; border-radius:3px; box-shadow:0 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.5),0 1px 10px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.15); margin: 1px; max-width:540px; min-width:326px; padding:0; width:99.375%; width:-webkit-calc(100% - 2px); width:calc(100% - 2px);\"><div style=\"padding:16px;\"> <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeW6Gd9k8W8/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" style=\" background:#FFFFFF; line-height:0; padding:0 0; text-align:center; text-decoration:none; width:100%;\" target=\"_blank\"> <div style=\" display: flex; flex-direction: row; alig